<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xinhai Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Xinhai Revolution was the revolution of 1911 that overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> and ended imperial rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It was triggered by a military mutiny at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang">Wuchang</a> and spread rapidly as provinces declared independence. It produced the abdication of the six-year-old emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> on 12 February 1912 and the establishment of the Republic of China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution.jpg" alt="Xinhai Revolution" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Opstanden,_revolutie,_SFA022823302.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The revolution came after a decade of agitation, revolts and uprisings. The Qing had struggled to reform and to resist foreign aggression since its defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> in 1842, and defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1895 convinced many of the need for institutional change. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a>'s Hundred Days' Reform of 1898 was cancelled by a conservative coup led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>. After 1900 conservatives at court thought the reforms too radical, and reformers thought them too slow. Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> feeling, which had never entirely disappeared, fed the revolutionaries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The flashpoint came on 10 October 1911 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang-uprising">Wuchang Uprising</a> by members of the New Army, after some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> units were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> to put down the Railway Protection Movement, a mass protest against the seizure of local railway ventures and their handover to foreign powers. Similar revolts followed, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a> on 22 October, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> on 29 October, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a> on 30 October and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> on 3 November, and in the end every province renounced the Qing. Violence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> occurred in several places. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, after the Manchu quarter fell on 24 October, about 20,000 Manchus in the city were killed.</p><p>On 1 November the Qing court appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> prime minister. Revolutionary forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> on 2 December, and a North–South Conference met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> on 18 December. On 29 December Sun Yat-sen was elected provisional president, and 1 January 1912 became the first day of the first year of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Sun agreed to resign in favour of Yuan if Yuan secured the emperor's abdication. The edict of abdication of the six-year-old emperor was promulgated on 12 February 1912, and Yuan was sworn in as provisional president on 10 March. Song Jiaoren, who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> to a majority in the first National Assembly elections, was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> on 20 March 1913 under a secret order by Yuan. Yuan proclaimed himself emperor in December 1915 but abdicated in March 1916 after strong opposition, and his death in June 1916 was followed by decades of warlordism.</p><p>The revolution ended over two millennia of imperial rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Tibet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/outer-mongolia">Outer Mongolia</a> took the fall of the dynasty as dissolving their allegiance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Bogd Khan</a> became ruler of the Bogd Khanate on 29 December 1911. 10 October is the National Day of the Republic of China on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and the Anniversary of the 1911 Revolution in China.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1911 Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Though it lasted only 14 years, the Qin dynasty is credited with inaugurating the Chinese imperial system, which persisted in some form until the <b>Xinhai Revolution</b> of 1911.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Xinhai Revolution</b> of 1911 began with a military uprising at Wuchang and spread rapidly as provinces declared independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 10 October 1911 an army mutiny at Wuchang, the Wuchang Uprising, set off the <b>Xinhai Revolution</b>, and within weeks most southern and central provinces had declared their independence from Beijing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Xinhai Revolution</b> of 1911–1912 Tibetan militia attacked the Qing garrison, and the Qing officials in Lhasa were forced to sign the Three Point Agreement for the surrender and expulsion of their forces from central Tibet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Such sentiments directly contributed to the success of the <b>Xinhai Revolution</b> in 1911.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Revolutionary soldiers mutinied at Wuchang, beginning the <b>Xinhai Revolution</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-10">10 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
